		<author><name><![CDATA[EA3V]]></name></author>
		<updated>2025-04-16T13:49:49</updated>

		<published>2025-04-16T13:49:49</published>
		<id>http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5065&amp;p=36775#p36775</id>
		<link href="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5065&amp;p=36775#p36775"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[DM-1701 deaf on DMR]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5065&amp;p=36775#p36775"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hello all, I acquired a DM-1701 last week. It worked fine with factory firmware. After I loaded the latest stable version of OpenGD77 (R20240908) the radio lost the ability to decode DMR audio from either of our local repeaters. One repeater is less than 3 air miles away and the other is less than 7 miles away. Both use Motorola hardware. One is networked to a c-Bridge and the other is connected to a Brandmeister server. The RSSI is at least 1/2 scale on both repeaters. The green LED lights but the radio does not decode audio. <br><br>The radio decodes DMR signals from my MMDVM hotspot without difficulty but of course that RF signal is much stronger than those of the repeaters.<br><br>An Anytone handheld on the same desk decodes audio from both repeaters without difficulty. When I reinstalled factory firmware, the DM-1701 was again able to decode repeater audio from the same location on my desk.</div></blockquote>The DMR system is very critical, and with a small frequency deviation, this is possible. Use an SDR to adjust the frequency on the Baofeng.<br>I'm assuming you're using the original Baofeng antenna. If you use an external base antenna, the Baofeng could saturate, causing this error.<br><br>EA3V 73<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=4014">EA3V</a> — Wed Apr 16, 2025 1:49 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
